> Cool! Monster definition! What do all this sort of things mean?

src/monst.c is your friends.  You'll find all the monster definitions in
there.  A quick rundown:

First is the monster name ("master lich"), then the monster symbol
(S_LICH == 'L').  Then the monster's stats in the LVL() block.  In
order, those are Difficulty level, movement rate, AC, Magic resistance,
and alignment.  After that are the generation and genocide flags.
Master liches have G_HELL (only generate in Gehennom/Endgame), G_GENO
(genocidable, I think), and G_NOCORPSE (don't leave a corpse).  Then,
the attacks in the A() block.  Each attack is either NO_ATTK (no attack,
duh), or an ATTK() block.  In an ATTK() block, you've got the attack
type, the attack damage-type, the number of dice of damage, and the type
of dice.  So a master lich has an AT_TUCH (touch) attack that does
AD_COLD (cold) type damage for 3d6.  Then the SIZ() block contains, I
think, the weight, nutritional value, extension length, sounds, and size
of the monster.  After that are the monster's resistances (fire, cold,
sleep, and poison for a master lich).  Then, the resistances conveyed by
eating the corpse (fire and cold).  Then a bunch of M1, M2, and M3
general attributes.  For instance, a master lich is humanoid, doesn't
need to breath, regens quickly, is hostile, is undead, has infravision,
etc ...  Finally, the color of the monster (HI_LORD == purple).

